# Line endings. Issue #53 — F-Droid could not build.
#
# The repo had no .gitattributes at all, so every file's line endings were
# whatever the committing machine's core.autocrlf happened to produce. The dev
# machine is Windows; CI and F-Droid's builders are Linux. The result, measured
# on tag v1.12.1: eleven tracked files stored with CRLF, and .gitignore stored
# with 70 of its 74 lines CRLF — which is exactly the "even parts of the files
# have different end-of-lines" the reporter saw.
#
# The one that actually broke the build: android/gradlew-quiet was stored with
# a CRLF shebang (`#!/bin/bash\r`). Linux passes the carriage return to the
# kernel as part of the interpreter path, so it looks for `/bin/bash\r`, does
# not find it, and fails with "bad interpreter: No such file or directory".
# CLAUDE.md documents `./gradlew-quiet` as this project's build command.
#
# Normalising is not enough on its own — without this file the next commit from
# a Windows checkout puts it straight back.

# Everything is text and is stored LF, whatever the working tree uses.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts genuinely need CRLF: cmd.exe mis-parses an LF .bat.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts are executed by a kernel that reads the shebang literally.
# Called out explicitly rather than relying on `text=auto` because these two
# are the ones whose breakage is silent and total.
gradlew        text eol=lf
gradlew-quiet  text eol=lf
*.sh           text eol=lf

# Binary. Never touched, never diffed as text. gradle-wrapper.jar in particular
# scanned as "3 CRLF lines" purely because its bytes happen to contain 0d 0a —
# normalising it would corrupt the wrapper and break every build.
*.jar    binary
*.png    binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff2  binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks    binary
